WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.030
- [Devin] Thanks to Skillshare

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:02.640
for keeping Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.580
Learn to think like a lawyer
for free for two months

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.400
by clicking the link in the dooply-doo.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.637
- I'm trying to be cool about this,

00:00:07.637 --> 00:00:09.690
but you can't just rip people's music off.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:10.660
It's against the law.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.550
- I am above the law.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.890
(Devin chuckles)

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.190
- A record company above law?

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:17.720
I've never heard anything like that.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.303
(bright music)

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.820
Hey, legal eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.830
and today, we are covering
the episode of South Park

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.130
that gave rise to the Chewbacca defense.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.320
This is probably one of the
most-quoted things by lawyers

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.860
in the entire world.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.140
And it's great to go
back and see the genesis

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.220
of the Chewbacca defense,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.000
because my friends and
I use it all the time.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.560
- Why would a Wookiee, an
eight-foot-tall Wookiee,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.560
want to live on Endor with a
bunch of two-foot-tall Ewoks?

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.410
That does not make sense.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.810
- As always, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection,

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.940
which I'll either sustain or overrule,

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.520
and stick around until
the end of the video

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.900
where I give the South Park

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.750
Chef Aid Chewbacca defense episode a grade

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.030
for legal realism.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.050
So, without further ado,

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.350
let's dig in to South Park Chef Aid.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.570
(bright music)

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.200
Okay, so, the background here

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.710
is that Chef wrote a song

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.852
called "Stinky Britches."

00:01:15.852 --> 00:01:17.119
♪ You got stinky britches ♪

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:18.470
♪ Stinky, stinky ♪
- What did you say?

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.730
- He's singing some new hit song.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.530
- I wrote that song 20 years ago.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.960
- And now it's being covered
by major rock artists,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.629
including Alanis Morissette...

00:01:26.629 --> 00:01:28.754
♪ Stinky, stinky britches ♪

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:30.120
♪ Stinky britches ♪

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.870
So, because he wrote the song,

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.760
he goes to the record company

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.570
to ask that he be credited

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:36.970
for having written the song.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.150
He actually doesn't ask
for monetary compensation.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.476
He just wants a credit for
having written the song...

00:01:42.476 --> 00:01:43.309
(sighs)

00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:44.434
"Stinky Britches."

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:45.407
♪ Stinky britches ♪

00:01:45.407 --> 00:01:47.460
♪ You got those stinky britches ♪

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.537
- So, you see, Mr. Big Record Producer,

00:01:49.537 --> 00:01:52.360
"Stinky Britches" was something
I wrote several years ago.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.440
- Hmm, I really see no resemblance

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.110
between that song and "Stinky Britches"

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.180
by our artist Alanis Morissette.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:59.065
- Huh?

00:01:59.065 --> 00:02:00.575
- It's the same (bleep) song.

00:02:00.575 --> 00:02:01.960
- I don't think that's the kind of song

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.050
that Alanis Morissette would write.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.500
Also, I don't know how
people are able to determine

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.610
that this is the same song,

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.910
given just the two seconds of
the song that they're playing,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.500
and it shares just, really, this lyric.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.510
I mean, presumably, this
song is exactly the same,

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.200
so I think we can understand

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.760
that it is supposed to be the same song,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.120
but understand that in the real world,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.880
just because the same
lyric is in multiple songs

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.090
does not necessarily mean

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.410
that copyright infringement has occurred.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:31.243
- I'm trying to be cool about this,

00:02:31.243 --> 00:02:33.710
but you can't just rip people's music off.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:34.680
It's against the law.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:35.557
- I am above the law.

00:02:35.557 --> 00:02:37.900
(Devin chuckles)

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.200
- A record company above the law?

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.980
I've never heard anything like that.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.510
- Mr. Chef, I'm afraid you
leave me no alternative.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:45.830
We're going to sue you.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.100
- Sue me?

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.770
You stole one of my songs,
and you're gonna sue me?

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:50.603
- Yes.

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.810
I suggest you get a real good lawyer.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.460
We'll have the best in the business.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.540
- We'll get my dad to be Chef's lawyer.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.029
- Yeah, and he's Jewish.

00:02:58.029 --> 00:02:59.000
- God.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.330
Okay, so, that might sound insane,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.290
that someone wrote a song,

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:07.010
that a music label then copied that song,

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.020
and the music label sues the person

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.520
who originally wrote the song.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.100
There is, actually, a real
mechanism in real life

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.410
that would allow that to happen.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.610
There's something called
declaratory relief

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.220
that allows someone,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.010
that once a controversy has occurred,

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.120
to then go to court.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.000
While they're effectively the plaintiff,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.880
really what they're doing is saying

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.470
I'm worried that this other
person is going to sue me

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.080
or that there is some controversy

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.510
that needs to be
adjudicated, and as a result,

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.750
I'm going to take the
affirmative step of filing suit

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.030
and just adjudicating this thing.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.490
So, while it might seem
crazy that the victim

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:46.110
could be the defendant
in a law suit like this,

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.620
it's actually totally plausible

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.100
that the music company

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.740
might be so worried about this lawsuit

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.190
that they take the affirmative step

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.330
of filing suit against Chef,

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.720
and the mechanism that they would be using

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.630
is called declaratory relief.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:00.583
So, actually could happen.

00:04:02.117 --> 00:04:04.344
♪ Stinky, stinky britches ♪

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:05.763
♪ Stinky britches ♪

00:04:05.763 --> 00:04:07.270
You know, I actually don't think

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:10.410
that that Alanis Morissette's
"Stinky Britches" song

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:14.000
sounds like the one that
Chef sang earlier on

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:14.917
in this episode.

00:04:14.917 --> 00:04:15.864
♪ Stinky britches ♪

00:04:15.864 --> 00:04:17.930
♪ You got those Stinky Britches ♪

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.310
Of course, we've seen in the news recently

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.143
that there have been

00:04:21.143 --> 00:04:22.880
some major copyright infringement actions

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.240
between songs that I don't think sound

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.240
anything like each other,

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.370
and frankly I think the
juries got it wrong.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:29.780
For example, we saw in

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.310
the "Blurred Lines" Marvin Gaye lawsuit

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.020
and the Katy Perry "Dark Horse" lawsuit,

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.900
both of which the juries
found for the plaintiff.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.760
I don't think that there
is a really good reason

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.130
why that should count as
copyright infringement.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:44.610
I've been meaning to do a video on this,

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:45.443
but in the meantime,

00:04:45.443 --> 00:04:47.880
check out Adam Neely's
videos on these subjects.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.210
He's done a really good job
of breaking down the lawsuits

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:51.850
from a music theory perspective,

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.550
and I think my hunch is right

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:56.900
that these lawsuits should not
have held for the plaintiff,

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.880
let alone penalized the
defendants by millions of dollars.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.800
- Now, just let me do
all the talking, Chef.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.570
We're gonna bring these (beep) down.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:04.590
- Right.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.530
- This court is now in session.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.000
Who is representing the defense?

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:07.833
- I am, your honor.

00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:09.050
Gerald Broflovski.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:10.710
- And representing the prosecution?

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:11.960
- I am, your honor.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:12.882
Johnnie Cochran.

00:05:12.882 --> 00:05:13.715
(crowd gasps)

00:05:13.715 --> 00:05:14.548
- Uh oh.

00:05:14.548 --> 00:05:15.460
- Why uh oh?

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.710
- Chef, that's Johnnie Cochran.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.270
He's the guy who got O.J. off.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.890
- Uh-oh.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.450
- All right, so, for the
younger viewers out there,

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:23.283
that is true,

00:05:23.283 --> 00:05:25.790
that there was a famous
lawyer named Johnnie Cochran

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:29.720
who was the lead counsel in
the O.J. Simpson murder trial.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.200
O.J. Simpson, for those of
you that don't remember,

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.280
was a star football player

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:37.670
and was alleged to have murdered his wife

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.130
and his wife's lover.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.960
Famously, O.J. Simpson had
many different lawyers,

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.740
but the most famous and lead
counsel was Johnnie Cochran

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.160
who was well-known for coming up

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.070
with phrases that would be
repeated throughout the trial.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.690
When I covered the Seinfeld finale

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.060
that involved a trial,

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.300
they also created a
parody of Johnnie Cochran,

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.110
whose name was Jackie Childs,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:01.990
who exhibited similar
flamboyance in court.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:03.680
- I am shocked and chagrined.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.480
- I think we're going to
see a lot of some parodies

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.270
of Johnnie Cochran in
this South Park trial.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.940
- And so on this 15th day
of what is considered to be

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.490
the most important trial of the day,

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:18.300
Johnnie Cochran has appeared
to defend Capitalist Records.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.600
The question now is will Cochran use

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.570
his famous Chewbacca Defense?

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:26.130
- So, famously, for some
reason, the O.J. Simpson trial,

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.220
they allowed cameras
inside of the courtroom,

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.390
and it just turned into this phenomenon

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.760
that the entire nation was obsessed with.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.510
I actually remember in
middle school, I think.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.410
We stopped classes.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.050
We rolled in a T.V. into the classroom

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.140
and we watched the verdict being read

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:45.020
because we knew it was going
to be a historical event,

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:46.210
and as a result,

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.020
the news coverage about
the O.J. Simpson trial,

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:53.130
and, of course, the Chef
trial in this particular case,

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.250
were just insane,

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:56.010
and there were cameras and talking heads.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:57.400
It was really sort of the beginning

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.370
of the cable news sort of
punditry that we see today.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:01.640
- What's a Chewbacca defense?

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:02.473
- I don't know.

00:07:02.473 --> 00:07:04.003
- That's what Cochran used
in the O.J. Simpson trial.

00:07:04.003 --> 00:07:05.760
- (beep) I hate that Cochran guy.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:06.630
If he was here in front of me,

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.304
I'd be like, hey!

00:07:08.304 --> 00:07:09.878
You stupid son of a (beep).

00:07:09.878 --> 00:07:11.849
(sputters incoherently)

00:07:11.849 --> 00:07:13.436
I may kick you in the nuts.

00:07:13.436 --> 00:07:15.570
- I'm sure that would scare
the hell out of him, Cartman.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:16.950
- Yeah, so,

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.250
what Cartman has done here

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:19.850
is a pretty good parody

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:21.970
of what I see all the time on Twitter,

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.700
which is people who
don't have a law degree

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.610
or any experience in the legal profession

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:29.300
second-guessing seasoned professionals.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.960
- And so in summation, ladies
and gentlemen of the jury.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:34.370
- Oh, okay, we're already in
summation for some reason.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.130
Apparently, the trial was the next day

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:39.730
and we're starting with closing arguments

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.270
instead of actually
going through the trial

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.720
and there is no discovery.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:47.020
This kind of trial would
take, conservatively,

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:50.040
two years to get to trial,
if it even got to trial,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.650
and 99% of cases do not even get to trial.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.270
They settle or they
are dismissed early on,

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:00.310
and they come nowhere near the
actual courtroom or a jury,

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.330
so, this is very, very wrong.

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:03.670
Very wrong.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:05.390
- You've heard the version
of my client's song

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:07.240
recorded over 20 years ago.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.650
You've heard the exact same
song produced by these cheats

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:11.640
in the past month.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.230
I'd say it's pretty much
an open-and-shut case.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:15.550
Make the right decision.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:16.880
Thank you.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.870
- And, you know, if you
had a very strong case

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.930
and you really thought that the two songs

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.630
sounded identical to each other,

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.140
there's no reason that
your closing argument

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:27.330
needs to be particularly fancy.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.650
You could just play them back-to-back

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:30.610
or next to each other,

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:33.610
and that might be very
persuasive evidence.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.980
But, again, if you had a very strong case,

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:38.760
it's very unlikely to get to trial.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:39.593
That's actually one thing

00:08:39.593 --> 00:08:41.820
that people often forget about trials

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:44.420
is that only marginal cases go to trial,

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:47.200
and that's both on a civil
level and a criminal level,

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.790
because it has to be a really close call,

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:51.240
otherwise it would not get

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.450
to that point in the judicial system.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:56.250
So, when you have an open-and-shut case,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.490
it's gonna be dealt with long
before you ever get to trial.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.960
- Mr. Johnnie Cochran,
your closing arguments.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:04.220
- Ladies and gentlemen
of the supposed jury.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:05.110
- The supposed jury.
- The defense attorney

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:06.620
would certainly want you
to believe that his client

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:08.710
wrote "Stinky Britches" 10 years ago,

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:09.800
and they make a good case.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.440
- Hell, I almost felt pity myself,

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.600
but ladies and gentlemen
of this supposed jury,

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.170
I have one final thing
I want you to consider.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:19.141
Ladies and gentlemen, this is Chewbacca.

00:09:19.141 --> 00:09:21.670
Chewbacca is a Wookiee
from the planet Kashyyyk,

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.490
but Chewbacca lives on the planet Endor.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:25.730
- That's true.
- Now think about that.

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:27.903
That does not make sense.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:29.633
- Dammit.
- What?

00:09:29.633 --> 00:09:31.530
- He's using the Chewbacca Defense.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:35.440
- So, this is a parody of
Johnnie Cochran's famous saying

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.440
during the O.J. Simpson trial.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.030
- If it doesn't fit,

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.100
you must acquit.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.790
- Now, in the actual O.J. Simpson trial,

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.420
there was a bloody glove
found at the crime scene.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:46.930
Both the prosecution and defense

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.540
made a very big deal about
this particular bloody glove,

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.050
and in one of the biggest
blunders of all-time

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:55.480
as prosecutors,

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:58.180
the prosecutors had O.J.
Simpson take the stand,

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:01.200
even though he was taking the
fifth as to everything else,

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.160
and they had him try on the glove.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:07.570
Now, they gave him plenty
of forewarning to know

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:09.230
that he was going to
be asked to put it on,

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:13.900
and he wore a latex glove
underneath the leather glove

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.360
as he was trying to try it on,

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.200
so he had to time to bulk up his hand

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.070
and he had the ability to
sort of spread out his fingers

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:23.270
and he was wearing a latex glove

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:24.890
so as to not contaminate it.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.920
And for all of those reasons,

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.970
the defense said that the
glove didn't fit O.J. Simpson,

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.470
and so the defense made
a huge deal out of this

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:34.620
in the closing argument.

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:36.500
And people make fun of Johnnie Cochran

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.650
for this childish saying

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.720
that if the glove doesn't
fit, you must acquit.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:43.530
But the thing is, it sticks with you.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:46.040
Even now, 20 years later,

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:49.090
we're still talking about
this particular defense

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:51.030
and it stuck with the jury, too.

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.860
I think it's actually
a stroke of brilliance

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.970
that Johnnie Cochran
was able to capitalize

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:57.460
on this bad fact

00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:59.320
that the prosecution misplayed their hand,

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:00.210
no pun intended,

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.043
and lo and behold,

00:11:01.043 --> 00:11:05.200
the jury did in fact find
O.J. Simpson to be not guilty.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:06.860
So, that is the underlying real-life thing

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:07.820
that actually happened

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:09.410
that gave rise to this parody version,

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:10.780
the Chewbacca Defense.

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:13.400
- Why would a Wookiee, an
eight-foot-tall Wookiee,

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:16.400
want to live on Endor with a
bunch of two-foot-tall Ewoks?

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:18.600
That does not make sense.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:19.433
But more importantly,

00:11:19.433 --> 00:11:20.550
you have to ask yourself

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:23.483
what does this have to do with this case?

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:25.630
Nothing.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:28.450
Ladies and gentlemen, it has
nothing to do with this case.

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:30.200
It does not make sense.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:31.033
Look at me.

00:11:31.033 --> 00:11:32.810
I'm a lawyer, defending
a major record company,

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:34.430
and I'm talking about Chewbacca.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:35.263
- Yeah.

00:11:35.263 --> 00:11:36.096
- Does that make sense?

00:11:36.096 --> 00:11:38.180
Ladies and gentlemen, I
am not making any sense.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:39.410
None of this makes sense,

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:40.740
and so you have to remember,

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.400
when you're in that jury room deliberating

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.260
and conjugating the
Emancipation Proclamation,

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:47.220
does it make sense?

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.053
No.

00:11:48.053 --> 00:11:49.750
Ladies and gentlemen
of this supposed jury,

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:51.630
it does not make sense.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:54.230
If Chewbacca lives on
Endor, you must acquit.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:57.020
- Okay, so, there are a lot of problems

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.701
with this particular theme

00:11:59.701 --> 00:12:03.510
and this parody of the
O.J. Simpson glove defense.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.670
Number one, Chewbacca, I think,

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:09.500
was on Endor for like the Battle of Endor,

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.120
and I don't know that he
actually lives on Endor.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:15.650
He, you know, goes around
the galaxy with Han Solo,

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:17.160
so I'm not even sure it's accurate to say

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:20.340
that Chewbacca actually lives on Endor.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:21.930
He's from Kashyyyk, obviously.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.200
But, let's put that aside

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:24.810
and let's focus on the actual legal issues

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.010
and not the Star Wars implications.

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:28.440
I'm sure I'm gonna get tons of comments

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.240
about how I don't even understand

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:31.930
the Star Wars extended universe.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:34.310
This is just non sequitur
after non sequitur.

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:36.540
It's literally trying to take something

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:37.480
that doesn't make sense

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:39.720
and saying, for no reason whatsoever,

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.310
that if that doesn't make sense,

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:45.310
that this jury should not
find in favor of Chef.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:46.310
- The defense rests.

00:12:48.262 --> 00:12:49.811
- Okay, then.

00:12:49.811 --> 00:12:51.040
(Devin laughs)

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:51.873
- So, yeah.

00:12:51.873 --> 00:12:54.000
Even the judge recognizes
that it's total gibberish

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.160
and makes no sense whatsoever,

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.570
and just because it doesn't make sense,

00:12:57.570 --> 00:12:59.000
that doesn't actually give a reason

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.280
for why the jury should
find for the plaintiff

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:02.560
in this particular case.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:03.980
But that being said,

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:08.280
it has made a huge mark
on lawyers in general.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:10.360
My friends and I will often say

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.230
we are using the Chewbacca Defense

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:14.520
or that opposing counsel is
using the Chewbacca Defense

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:15.970
when they're doing something

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:17.780
that doesn't make any sense at all.

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:18.780
Doesn't really work.

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:19.740
Judges don't like it.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:20.880
Juries don't believe it,

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:24.220
and it doesn't work out
the way you think it would.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:25.530
- How find you the jury?

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:29.030
- We find the defendant,
Jerome Chef McElroy,

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:30.129
guilty as charged.

00:13:30.129 --> 00:13:31.250
(attendees gasp)

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:32.810
- And that doesn't even make any sense

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:33.703
because this is a civil case.

00:13:33.703 --> 00:13:36.530
This is a civil case for
a declaratory relief.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:37.960
It's not even a criminal case

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:39.560
where you could find someone guilty.

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:42.670
You can't find someone
guilty in a civil case.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:45.000
You would have to have a
criminal case for that.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.440
- Mr. Chef, you've been found guilty

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.160
of harassing a major record label.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.120
- Harassing.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:51.410
- The full fee of two million
dollars will be handed over

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:52.460
within 24 hours.

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:54.540
- Do I look like I have
two million dollars?

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:56.210
- Well, you have 24 hours to find it

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:57.690
or else you'll have to go to jail

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.540
for eight million years.

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:00.540
- Also not a thing.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.550
Yeah, okay, so a number of
things are very wrong here.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:06.740
Number one, you can't
sentence someone to go to jail

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:07.573
in a civil case.

00:14:07.573 --> 00:14:09.360
You can't find them
guilty in a civil case.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.590
What Chef was doing was
in no way harassment

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:16.440
and wouldn't even be grounds
for anything like that.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:17.720
On top of that, you wouldn't be given

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:19.400
a choice between going to jail

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:20.640
and paying a huge fine,

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:22.720
even if it was a criminal case,

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.157
and frankly, this is probably
one of the biggest issues

00:14:25.157 --> 00:14:26.430
in civil law,

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:29.000
is that if there was a judgment
for two million dollars

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.070
against some poor individual

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:33.730
who's just a chef at a local school,

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:34.910
they're not going to be able to pay that,

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:36.740
so they'd effectively be judgment proof,

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.350
and they'd just file for bankruptcy

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:40.930
and discharge the debt that is owed.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:41.920
- And that chair, too.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.089
I want that chair.

00:14:43.089 --> 00:14:44.630
- Hey, that's my favorite chair!

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:45.520
- You heard the judge.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.540
Since you lost the case,

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:49.030
I can seize whatever I
want to pay my legal fees.

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:50.840
Yeah, take that water cooler, too.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:53.410
- So, while this timeline
is totally unrealistic,

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:56.460
what is actually realistic is, sometimes,

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.540
if you have a judgment in court,

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:02.280
you can use that to get all of the things

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.650
of the person who the debt is against.

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.650
Or you can get a wage garnishment

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.230
so that if they have a salary,

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:09.510
you can get a percentage,

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.050
sometimes a very high percentage,

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:13.530
of that salary in order to pay back

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:14.770
the debt that is owed.

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:17.930
And in order to get the court to sign off

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:19.900
on turning over physical property,

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:22.190
you would need something
called a writ of attachment,

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:23.600
which is something that would happen

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:26.210
in the months after the trial,

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:28.520
saying that you are owed this judgment

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:29.500
and the only way to get it

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:32.940
is to basically take
the physical possessions

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.030
of the defendant or the
plaintiff, in this case.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:35.890
The funny thing is,

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:37.580
if you're able to get
a writ of attachment,

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:42.230
you get the sheriff of the
city or the municipality

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:43.250
to enforce it,

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:47.480
so law enforcement will
actually go to the house

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:49.630
of the person against
whom you have a judgment

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:52.190
and they will help you
take all of the things

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:55.210
and make sure that the person
is not trying to stop you,

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:57.610
so, presumably in this particular case,

00:15:57.610 --> 00:15:58.810
there would have been a writ of attachment

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:01.290
and the physical goods
would have been sold

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:02.310
to discharge the debt.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:03.210
It's a real thing.

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:06.400
- Ladies and gentlemen
of the supposed jury,

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.890
you must now decide whether
or not to reverse the decision

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:10.240
for my client Chef.

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:13.410
I know he seems guilty,
but ladies and gentlemen,

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:15.490
this is Chewbacca.

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:16.620
- Okay, hold on.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:18.340
Before Johnnie Cochran gets into

00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:19.890
the second Chewbacca Defense,

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:21.090
let's think like a lawyer.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:24.250
(energetic rock music)

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:27.720
There's no such thing as a
jury reversing the decision

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.440
of another jury.

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:32.360
There's a principle in the
law called res judicata

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:35.090
that says once a case is determined,

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:35.923
that's it.

00:16:35.923 --> 00:16:36.756
It's done.

00:16:36.756 --> 00:16:38.800
And in the case of a jury trial,

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:41.950
you might be able to get the
appeals court to weigh in

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.920
and say that there was some
defect from a legal perspective,

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:47.390
but you would never be
able to get a second jury

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:50.100
on exactly the same factual matter

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:52.370
to render another factual decision.

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:55.810
That's absolutely not allowed at all.

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:57.780
Once the facts are settled,

00:16:57.780 --> 00:16:58.970
they're settled.

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:00.200
- Now, think about that for one minute.

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.960
That does not make sense.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:03.140
Why am I talking about Chewbacca

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:04.430
when a man's life is on the line?

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:05.263
Why?

00:17:05.263 --> 00:17:06.096
I tell you why.

00:17:06.096 --> 00:17:07.000
I don't know.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:08.270
It doesn't make sense.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:11.360
If Chewbacca does not make
sense, you must acquit.

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:12.260
Here, look at the monkey.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:13.276
Look at the silly monkey.

00:17:13.276 --> 00:17:15.010
(man groans)
(head explodes)

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:16.141
- Ah.

00:17:16.141 --> 00:17:19.020
It's totally unrealistic,
but it's really funny.

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:20.508
Look at the silly monkey.
- Look at thee silly monkey.

00:17:20.508 --> 00:17:23.091
(bright music)

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:25.260
- All right, now it's time

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:27.500
to give the South Park
Chewbacca defense episode

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:29.401
a grade for legal realism.

00:17:29.401 --> 00:17:32.290
(gavel pounding)

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:34.380
On the one hand, this episode
sort of hints at things

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:37.160
that you don't really see
in legal dramas very much

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:38.130
like writs of attachment

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:39.350
and declaratory relief

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:40.490
where a defendant sues a plaintiff.

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:41.323
That's really cool.

00:17:41.323 --> 00:17:42.380
That doesn't happen very often.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.150
And the Chewbacca Defense

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:46.980
has been adopted by lawyers everywhere.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:48.250
We use it all the time.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:50.660
- On the other hand is
basically everything else.

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:52.500
The timing is all screwed up.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:55.370
The second trial makes no sense at all.

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:58.490
The legal arguments are
intentionally insane.

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:00.920
So, I'm gonna have to give
this episode of South Park

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:04.160
a C-minus for legal accuracy.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.379
It really needs to go
back to South Park High.

00:18:07.379 --> 00:18:08.960
(Cartman stammering incoherently)

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:10.573
- I may kick you in the nuts.

00:18:10.573 --> 00:18:13.070
